  "notes": "8-semester (finishes in 7 simulated terms at this credit load), bulletin-stated 126-127cr program (only one plan variant, no options to choose between). Scraped a new AIE department catalog (AIE 355, AIE 489W -- didn't exist before this build). Three real hidden-prereq/concurrent gaps found, none present anywhere in AIE's own bulletin plan: A-I 305 needs MATH 220 (hard, no alternative) and MATH 231/230 as prereqs; A-I 370 needs STAT 200/DS 200 as a prereq AND one of CMPSC 465/DS 305/CMPSC 462 concurrently (found via a real engine bug hunt -- prereqs_satisfied passed but concurrent_satisfied silently failed, which is what actually diagnoses this class of gap, not just reading the catalog's prereq_groups). Added MATH 220 (2cr, sem2), MATH 231 (2cr, sem3), STAT 200 (4cr, sem1), and CMPSC 462 (3cr, sem4 -- cheapest of the three concurrent options since its own prereqs are already satisfied by CMPSC 132/360/MATH 141 elsewhere in this same plan) to unlock them, same pattern as every other hidden-prereq-chain addition this session -- pushes the computed total to ~137-138cr, documented rather than silently absorbed. Bumped max_credits_per_semester to 21 to keep the resulting load realistic given the additions. 'CMPSC 131 or 121 and DS 120' modeled as two separate items (3cr + 1cr) per real catalog data. Verified 2026-08-27: no dedicated AIE handbook exists yet (brand-new program, no PDF handbook found at eecs.psu.edu or elsewhere; administered by the Dept. of Computer Science and Engineering). The 9 'Technical Elective' slots have no enumerated course list on the bulletin (confirmed absent from every source checked) -- wired via open_elective scoped to the plan's own technical departments (A-I/AIE/CMPSC/DS/MATH/PHYS/STAT/EE), excluding ENGL/CAS, as the most defensible practical default given no real denylist/allowlist is published. The 'AIE Department List' slot is left as a generic unfilled placeholder on purpose: the bulletin explicitly calls it a *non-technical* elective, but the plan's departments list is all-technical, so wiring it via open_elective would produce technical picks that contradict the handbook's own stated intent -- left unwired rather than guessing at a broader department list not evidenced anywhere. Fixed real discrepancy: the bulletin's own Additional Courses table lists 'CMPSC 448 or CMPSC 445' as an OR-pair (Semester 5) -- was hardcoded to only CMPSC 448. Also found two more real, independent prereq gaps: CMPSC 448 (needed for A-I 410) requires STAT 319 or STAT 415, neither present -- added STAT 414 (Semester 4, satisfiable via MATH 231) then STAT 415 (Semester 5). CMPSC 462 (added above to unlock A-I 370's concurrent requirement) itself has a fourth AND-group requiring CMPSC 330, not present -- added CMPSC 330 (Semester 3, satisfiable via CMPSC 122/132 alone). Added the real MATH 21 fallback chain item (Semester 3) for a hypothetical student with no reported math placement. Removed MATH 3 (Semester 1) and MATH 4 (Semester 2), previously added on the theory that MATH 21 needs MATH 4 needs MATH 3: math_catalog.json's own MATH 21 entry has an empty prereq_groups (no prerequisite at all), and MATH 3/MATH 4 are separately hardcoded as unconditionally satisfied everywhere by expand_math_placement regardless of any student input -- they were dead weight, verified via build_full_plan to have zero effect on schedulability either way.",
